data('MEMORY-SPIKES',14,'47 transfer function;67 weak fluctuation;77 preferred angle','16–28 activation jump/decay;40–77 rate feedback and compensated count noise;79–88 fixed family','30–38 five chosen spike times and50ms decay;77 population replication fixes mean and divides input variance byn','49 conditional independent generation;75 Gaussian aggregate input only;77 leading largeN;88 tuned family',92,'Synaptic feedback taught foundations11–16; Poisson and conditional limits at39–84; Brownian persistence74','Main7–11 and SI1–6 define these equations. Conditional count mean and covariance give compensated moments; independent Brownian increments reproduce them to leading weak-noise order. Each coefficient sqrt(r) times dB has dimensionless activation units. Replicated weights1/n give mean n/n and variance n/n². Steady noise-free balance gives sbar=tau rbar.','Jump spikes and Gaussian input fluctuations are exact process versus controlled approximation; criteria: aggregate-small input and fixed mean dynamics. General W/phi are retained; no optional neural model is made mandatory.')
data('MEMORY-LINEARIZATION',92,'94 linearization;125 right zero vector;127–140 left zero vector and normalization','94–147: Taylor cancellation, tangent derivative Ku=0 and vTK=0 remove stable modes','149–171: xdot=ay,ydot=-lambda y gives xinf=x0+(a/lambda)y0;figure a/lambda=.5,y0=2 gives shift1','110 fixed-state noise;125 other modes decay;147 symmetric W need not make K symmetric',175,'Fixed-point family at79–88; Taylor/linear algebra baseline; persistent/decaying directions taught persistence9–54','SI7–15 p3 and16–18 p4 agree with K=-I/tau+diag(phi prime)W. Differentiate F(sbar)=0 to get Ku=0. If Kz=lambda z and vTK=0, then vTz=0 for lambda nonzero. Integrating y0 exp(-lambda t) gives the displayed endpoint; editable figure marks and caption agree.','Dynamical left-vector projection versus perpendicular projection answer the same surviving-shift question; criteria: actual flow endpoint. The tilted example shows why ordinary perpendicular geometry is insufficient.')
data('MEMORY-DIFFUSION',175,'205–211 multi-coordinate normalization and diffusion tensor','175–193 project independent increments;205–211 matrix covariance','182–193 covariance contraction: sum_ij v_i v_j sqrt(r_i r_j)delta_ij dt=sum_i v_i²r_i dt','198–203 local and leading weak noise;200 enough spikes;201 nearly fixed coefficients',215,'Left/right projection at112–171; Brownian increment covariance persistence74 and dynamics75','SI14–15 p3 directly gives2D=sum v²r. SI21–27 pp5–6 supplies coupled coordinates. Multiplying independent increment covariance by dual projection vectors gives2D_alpha beta=sum_i v_i^alpha r_i v_i^beta, allowing cross-coordinate covariance. This is a local variance result, not an exact mean-drift computation.','Scalar versus multi-coordinate projection share the covariance mechanism; criteria: represented dimension and possible coupling. Local versus long-time behavior is deliberately deferred to the density chapter.')
data('MEMORY-SYMMETRIC',215,'217 gprime/rprime; earlier transfer-function definition47','217–248: derivative identities make gprime a left-zero candidate and normalization supplies tau','256–279 mutual inhibition: u=(.5,-.5),v=(1,-1),2D=b/tau; b20,tau.1 gives D100','248 invertible transfer where used;250–252 symmetry, attracting family and noise model;269 endpoints',279,'General projection and D at112–211; rate-input rule47','SI16–18 p4 has squared gprime numerator. K transpose acting on gprime vanishes using W symmetric and gprime=tau W rprime. Substitution v_i=gprime_i/[tau sum rprime gprime] gives the displayed squared denominator. Main2 visibly lacks the numerator square. Mutual-inhibition sum relaxes with difference neutral; explicit rates give total200/s andD100, matching figure.','General left-vector evaluation versus symmetric tuning expression use the same D; criteria: whether W_ij=W_ji. The mutual-inhibition case proves rotational/translation symmetry is unnecessary.')
data('MEMORY-LONG-TIME',11,'13 Ito and Fokker Planck;21 Dirac point mass','13–34 density on line/ring;48–85 test-function and moment derivation;87–94 linearD example','28–44 wrap Gaussian, integrate x²/(2pi) to pi²/3;87–94 mutual excitation gives D=theta/(4tau)','70,78 vanishing boundary terms and smoothD;85 valid domain;94 source factor discrepancy',98,'Local projected D at dynamics175–211; Brownian/variance conventions at persistence74–123','SI28–37 pp7–8 match p_t=(Dp)doubleprime, Gaussian variance2Dt and angular uniform plateau. Taylor test-function expectation DFdoubleprime then two integrations by parts gives the PDE. Moment derivatives are0 and2E[D]; another generator application gives E[DDdoubleprime] and t² coefficient DDdoubleprime. Mutual excitation rates sum2theta/tau and v=.5 give2D=theta/(2tau), agreeing SI41; SI40 differs by factor2.','Line versus wrapped coordinate keeps D fixed and changes error reporting; constant versus linearD shows state dependence need not curve variance. Criteria: boundaries and coefficient shape; no blanket all-delay law.')
data('MEMORY-DRIFT-SCOPE',98,'100 systematic drift;102 Stratonovich midpoint convention','100–106 distinguish microscopic mean, stochastic convention and covariance extension','102 with b=sqrt(2D),bbprime=Dprime, conversion bbprime/2=Dprime/2;104 compare dt/N and sqrt(dt/N)','100 exact drift requires jump process;102 scale only;104 decoder knows drift/domain;106 matching joint likelihood',110,'Diffusion spread already derived; Ito beginning evaluation13; largeN noise scaling dynamics77','SI Systematic drift pp4–5 explicitly says microscopic jumps are needed. Differentiating sqrt(2D) gives bbprime=Dprime, so conversion term is Dprime/2. With D order1/N, SD increment scales sqrt(dt/N), drift displacement dt/N. vTQv is covariance propagation; main Discussion p5 calls more general correlated-noise models conjectural.','Ito versus Stratonovich with the same written zero drift are different processes, not interchangeable notation. Mean subtraction versus random variance removal are distinct tasks; criteria: actual microscopic dynamics and known decoder correction.')
data('MEMORY-FIGURES',110,'112 activity bump ordered by preferred angles','110–126 interpret retained-flow projection, stored-state movement and finite-spike readout','119–126 sourceFig2 ring versus mutual inhibition;C tests predicted D and bound,D wrapped long delay','123 dashed curve is lower bound;126 simulation not animal data',128,'Flow projection dynamics94–171; angular plateau long-delays28–34','Personally inspected source mainPDF pp2–3 figures/captions in Stage1; unchanged source raster hashes bound in Stage3. Fig1A flow endpoints define coordinate. Fig1B separates storage movement from finite-spike uncertainty. Fig2C solid theory versus simulations and dashed sigmoidal bound;D wraps differences to(-pi,pi), consistent pi²/3;E–G mutual inhibition supplies a nonrotational design.','Ring and mutual-inhibition simulations test the same analytical diffusion calculation under different architecture; criteria: agreement with D and boundary convention, not empirical proof of where animals store memory.')
data('MEMORY-FI',11,'13 likelihood;32 score;40 information/rate;60 unbiased;76 Cramer Rao/prior','13–46 log likelihood, squared score and Poisson covariance;60–76 inverse-information error proof','50–58 baseline5 versus25Hz with same changing Gaussian component; peak slope zero and information lower on flanks','46 positive-rate/regularity;76 locally unbiased finite-sample limits, prior/boundaries/ambiguity',80,'Poisson counts foundations39–84; tuning slope foundations18; covariance97; calculus and Cauchy Schwarz baseline','SI42–44 pp9–10 and main3 p2 define J. Differentiating log product yields score=sum(rprime/r)(n-rh); zero means and diagonal count variance give E[S²]=h sum rprime²/r. Differentiate unbiased estimator expectation then apply Cauchy Schwarz for Var>=1/(Jh). Adding20Hz baseline does not change derivative, so nonzero-slope information decreases; caption/alt and editable curves agree.','Internal-state versus sensory FI use the same algebra only for corresponding tuning; unbiased local error bound versus actual finite-spike estimator attainment are different claims. Criteria: rates, likelihood and assumptions.')
data('MEMORY-BOUND',80,'92 comparison vectors;115 proportionality;179 matrix PSD order','84–129 Cauchy Schwarz and sufficient exponential equality;171–193 residual matrix proof','133–141 zero-bias linear equality family;145–169 balanced/shifted mutual inhibition gives bounds1000 and750 with actualD1000','111 model-specific;129 sufficient not necessary;185 distinguishable coordinates/Jinvertible',200,'General D and normalized left tangent dynamics175–211; J and score at information13–76','SI45–56 pp10–12. Dot(a,b)=sum v rprime=1/tau; squared norms2D,J give product lower bound. Equality v=(rprime/r)/(tau J). Zero-bias excitation yields2D=theta/(2tau),J=2/(tau theta), hence exact saturation without exponential transfer. At b200,tau.1,theta0 rates1000 andJ.05;theta100 rates1500,500 andJ1/15, bound750. Q=V-U(UtU)^-1 gives QtQ=2D-tau^-2 J^-1.','Attained lower bound versus actual minimum under resources are distinct. Exponential transfer is sufficient; the linear family tests necessity. Scalar-coordinate checks do not replace the coupled matrix inequality.')
data('MEMORY-COMPARISONS',200,'210 tuning family;217 width/resource constraints;232 radial symmetry','202–238 scale the diffusion/information sums and change variables in the tuning integral','206 fixed rate tuning gives tau^-2 but fixed activation tuning tau^-1;232–238 width powers differ with dimension and firing budget','204 geometry/attractor preserved;210 away boundaries;232 matrix equality/radial tuning;238 sparse coverage/baseline/correlations',240,'Information bound and matrix inverse at80–193; local rate/activation difference dynamics256–269','Mainpp2,4 tuning/resource discussion plus guide derivation. Numerator N and denominatorN² give D inverseN; J sum givesN. Common rate scalea gives Jscalea. Width slope² contributes w^-2 and volume w^d, so Jentries scale w^(d-2); radial shape/uniform centers cancel cross terms and equal diagonals. Saturated bound inverts this power; total firing fixed requires a~w^-d and gives J~w^-2.','Compare N,rate,tau,width only after saying held resources; alternatives peak rate versus total firing change the conclusions. Criteria: actual diffusion under preserved tuning/model, not a universal narrow-versus-broad rule.')
data('MEMORY-READOUT-SETUP',11,'13 s,e and cue x;21 readout translation invariance;44 Gaussian observation bins','13–32 error sum and conditional covariance proof;44–56 state increments plus noisy state observations','21 E[se]=E[s E(e|history)]=0;34–42 timeline fixes cue-state estimate, not observation endpoint','13 unbounded constantD/nodrift;21 locally unbiased/independentfuture;32 priors/boundaries/encoding;56 local Gaussian only',60,'Covariance foundations97–125; J information40–76; Brownian covariance persistence112–121','SI57 p12 gives storage+readout structure; SI58–63 p13 supplies independent Gaussian state/observation likelihood. Conditional zero mean makes cross covariance vanish. Shift-invariant readout variance is independent of cue state, giving2DT+Vread. Gaussian observation variance1/(Jh) follows informative-sample approximation, not arbitrarily sparse physical bins.','Storage error versus reading cue-state error are additive only under the stated conditions. State y observation is equivalent spike information rather than another sensor; criteria: estimator target and allowed postcue observations.')
data('MEMORY-NAIVE',60,'60 equal-weight/naive estimator;62 B_D Brownian state displacement','62–106 retain min(s,t) covariance in a double integral then optimize duration','74 count variance1/(JL);80–85 split integral into triangles giving L³/3;99 optimum sqrt(3/(2DJ))','56 Gaussian equivalent model and independent observation error;60 stationary-assumption decoder applied to movingstate',113,'Brownian covariance persistence112–121; Gaussian observation model44–56','SI76–81 pp15–16. Direct averaging n independent observation noises gives1/(JL). Brownian states share past increments, so double integral of min isL³/3 and motion variance2DL/3. Derivative2D/3-1/(JL²)=0 gives optimum; substituting yields(2/sqrt3)sqrt(2D/J).','Equal weighting versus motion-aware weighting address cue-state estimation; equal weighting reduces count noise but increasingly mixes displaced states. Criteria: variance versus duration; motion-aware solution follows next.')
data('MEMORY-IDEAL',113,'115 precision;142 tanh/artanh;161 coth','115–158 add likelihood precisions after backward transition variance; derive Pprime=J-2DP² and coth error','176–191 coth(1)=1.3130,coth(2)=1.0373,coth(3)=1.00497;ratio2/sqrt3=1.15470,SD1.07457','117 no informativeprior;123 vanishing bin convention;191 fullaccess characteristic saturation not abrupt cutoff',195,'Gaussian likelihood and independence44–56; variance addition foundations104; naive tradeoff60–109','SI58–75 pp13–15 yields the same ideal error. Multiplying Gaussian likelihoods adds inverse variances. Convolution with state increment adds2Dh before inversion, then earliest observation addsJh. Expansion gives Pprime=J-2DP²; separation gives artanh and tanh, reciprocal gives sqrt(2D/J)coth. SI71 printed plus sign disagrees with SI73 but this independent recursion matches SI75.','Naive equal weights versus ideal motion-aware weighting use the same data and error target. Criteria: finiteL variance, limiting benefit and implementation assumptions; the guide does not claim abrupt zero benefit aftertau.')
data('MEMORY-PARTIAL',195,'197 independent retention probability alpha','197–212 replace each rate/derivative byalpha times original while keepingD','214–216 full L*.1s,V.001;alpha.1 gives.316228s,.00316228;naive.173205s and.547723s;storage.020','212 arbitrary subset need not have alpha information;216 storage noise remains',220,'Poisson FI formula information40–46; ideal/naive duration scales99,173','SI82 andFigS2 p16. Substitution(alpha rprime)²/(alpha r)=alpha times contribution; original network stochastic dynamics do not change when observer misses spikes. L and limiting variance therefore scalealpha^-1/2. Direct numbers and2D x2seconds reproduce totals.021 and.0231623.','Full versus random partial spike access holds the memory network fixed; criterion: duration/error of readout. A biased subset of neurons is not automatically an alpha fraction of information.')
data('MEMORY-EXTENSIONS',220,'222 rate relaxation withtaum;256 independent Gaussian strengthq','224–254 two-block left-vector normalization;256–264 matched Gaussian likelihood and projection','239 b=taum a, normalization gives a_i=gprime_i/[(tau+taum)sumgprime rprime];262 J=sumrprime²/q and2D=qsumv²','230 symmetric/noise placement;254 only displayed two-stage model;262 pointwise equality not universal linear preference',268,'Left projection dynamics127–193; equality information115–141; Gaussian observation likelihood40–76','SI84–92 pp17–18: lower-right rate block must be-I/taum by SI84, despite SI85 printed plus. Leftblock equation gives b=taum a; tangent pairing gives tau+taum. Gaussian subsectionSI93–95 p19 changes noise law; keepingq explicit gives matched J,D and same Cauchy Schwarz product, with equalityv proportionalrprime.','One versus two time constants and Poisson versus constant Gaussian variance are different modeled assumptions; criteria: noise placement, normalized tangent and likelihood. No general biological sum-of-times guarantee is asserted.')
